40.6975,"d":3505,"ds":"The Sannai-Maruyama Site (Japanese: 三内丸山遺跡, Hepburn: Sannai-Maruyama iseki) is an archaeological site and museum located in the Maruyama and Yasuda neighborhoods to the southwest of central Aomori City in Aomori Prefecture in northern Japan, containing the ruins of a very large Jōmon period settlement.","wpu":"Sannai-Maruyama Site","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/140913_Sannai-Maruyama_site_Aomori_Japan01bs6bs6.jpg/960px-140913_Sannai-Maruyama_site_Aomori_Japan01bs6bs6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Aomori Museum of Art","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":40.8073,"lo":140.7009,"d":3516,"ds":"The Aomori Museum of Art (青森県立美術館, Aomori Kenritsu Bijutsukan) is a museum in Aomori, Japan, opened in July 2006. It is located near Sannai-Maruyama Site, which the museum's design takes inspiration from in its partially-buried structure. The museum houses more than 120 works from drawings to three-dimensional works by Yoshitomo Nara, a young artist from Aomori Prefecture.","wpu":"Aomori Museum of Art","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/140913_Aomori_Museum_of_Art_Japan03n.jpg/960px-140913_Aomori_Museum_of_Art_Japan03n.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Nogiwa Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":40.85,"lo":140.675833,"d":5070,"ds":"Nogiwa Park (野木和公園, Nogiwa kōen) is an urban park in the western part of the city of Aomori, Japan. Major features of the park include a reservoir, various decorative trees, wetlands, several footbridges, and an observatory.","wpu":"Nogiwa Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Nogiwa_Park_Koj%C5%8D_%C5%8Chashi_%282%29.jpg/960px-Nogiwa_Park_Koj%C5%8D_%C5%8Chashi_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Seiryū-ji","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":40.821944,"lo":140.818333,"d":7486,"ds":"Seiryū-ji (青龍寺) is a Kōyasan Betsuin (affiliate temple) located in Aomori, Aomori Prefecture. The temple was founded by a Great Acharya Ryūkou Oda (織田隆弘), who later built Shōwa Daibutsu (昭和大仏) in 1984. Roughly 21.35 meters in height, it is the tallest seated bronze figure of Buddha in Japan.","wpu":"Seiryū-ji","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Seiry%C5%AB-ji.jpg/960px-Seiry%C5%AB-ji.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"New Aomori Prefecture General Sports Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":40.843349,"lo":140.839969,"d":9369,"ds":"New Aomori Prefecture General Sports Park (Japanese: 新青森県総合運動公園) is a group of sports facilities in Aomori, Aomori, Japan. The Kakuhiro Group Athletic Stadiums is an athletic stadium in Aomori, Aomori, Japan. The facilities are the home venue for the ReinMeer Aomori, a Japan Football League team.","wpu":"New Aomori Prefecture General Sports Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Field_and_spectator_stand_of_Shin-Aomori_Prefectural_Comprehensive_Athletic_Park_Athletics_Stadium_004.jpg/960px-Field_and_spectator_stand_of_Shin-Aomori_Prefectural_Comprehensive_Athletic_Park_Athletics_Stadium_004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Aomori , officially Aomori City (青森市, Aomori-shi), is the capital city of Aomori Prefecture, in the Tōhoku region of Japan. As of 1 August 2023, the city had an estimated population of 264,945 in 136,781 households, and a population density of 321 people per square kilometer spread over the city's total area of 824.61 km2 (318.38 sq mi). Aomori is one of Japan's 62 core cities and the core of the Aomori metropolitan area.","wpu":"Aomori","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Aomori_Montage.jpg/960px-Aomori_Montage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}